# campaigns/ — campaign packs

A **pack** is a campaign's reusable intelligence (copy, timing, playbook,
gotchas) saved as a directory so that months later "have we ever done
something like this?" is answered by `cs campaign packs`, and re-running one is copy-and-edit
— no new one-off script, no dedicated cron. **A pack is DATA + templates + prose
in this clone; the runner is kernel code** (`cs/campaign.py` `send_reminder` /
`send_sms`, loaded by `cs/campaign_pack.py`). This directory is empty on a fresh
clone and grows with use.

Layout (design brief §5.1b):

```
campaigns/<pack-name>/
├── campaign.toml      # [pack] kind (fixed-template | composed-draft) /
│                      #        description (what the discovery search reads) /
│                      #        campaign (the engine campaign name) / status
│                      #        (active | done) / ends_on (a date, or "never") /
│                      #        dates (prose) / confirm_question;
│                      #        [windows] optional overrides of [knobs]
│                      #        (reminder_after_hour, sms_hour, reminder_max)
├── mail_first.md      # first line `Subject: …`, blank line, then a markdown
├── mail_reminder.md   # body with {name} {company} … placeholders filled from
│                      # the contact row, rendered via send_mail's md→plain+html
├── sms.txt            # SMS text, same {placeholders}
├── builders.py        # OPTIONAL: build(row) / build_reminder(row) → (subject,
│                      # plain, html) for rich hand-built HTML (tel: links, dial
│                      # codes). Takes precedence over the templates.
├── playbook.md        # how it ran, the gotchas, the ground truth
└── legacy/            # OPTIONAL superseded one-off code, kept for reference,
                       # NEVER imported (the kernel runner supersedes it)
```

Gates (all kernel-enforced, unchanged): stamp-before-send,
`CS_PAUSE`, Sent-archive dedup first. A fixed-template action whose campaign has
**no pack** is refused loudly — the kernel never invents copy.

## When the campaign is over

**A finished campaign delivers nothing** — no first notice, no reminder, no
SMS, no composed draft — on every path, including the ones reached with a
contact id directly. Two fields say when it is over, and both are enforced:

- **`status`** — `active` or `done`, and nothing else (an unrecognised word
  refuses at load). `done` stops every delivery. **Flip it the day the
  campaign ends**; this is the field a human owns.
- **`ends_on`** — the backstop for the day nobody remembers to flip `status`.
  A date (`ends_on = 2026-07-31`) past which the pack refuses to deliver
  **even while `status = "active"`**. A campaign with no end says so
  explicitly: `ends_on = "never"`. Anything unreadable refuses at load — an
  end date that cannot be parsed is never read as "no limit".

`dates` is neither of those. It stays prose for whoever reads this pack in a
year ("first notice → cutover"), and it gates nothing.

A refusal is always visible: `cs campaign pending` reports
`delivery_blocked` with the reason and the date plus a `held` count of what
was withheld, and each sender returns the same sentence. Replies still come
through — a customer who wrote to you is owed an answer whether or not the
campaign is finished.

To reuse a past campaign for new numbers/dates:
`cp -r campaigns/<old> campaigns/<new>` → edit `campaign.toml` + templates →
seed the engine campaign + contacts → the general operator runs it through the
kernel runner.